think Read REMEMBER — the named next agent-doable crux is a .brush theme carried onto rung two's full painted graph (the reading ground), where rung four themed only the head accent. Lindy-first: the durable artifact is a themeable reading surface; crux-first: prove the whole ground flips, not just the accent.
think Studied the pattern — rung two mantra_browser_view.rye paints the full graph via tokens.dress_grid (paper/ink); rung four mantra_browser_theme.rye reads a .brush through parse_brush + collect_brush_style, infuses via tokens.resolve, paints via heads.paint_heads. The mode fixture dark-mode.brush already sets paper/ink only — the right theme for a ground recolor.
think Wrote pond/apps/mantra_browser_view_theme.rye — resolve_theme requires the theme name the paper token (ThemeMissingGround), infuses over base_sheet, and paints with rung two's unchanged view.paint_graph. Fully additive: imports mantra_browser, mantra_browser_view, tokens, skate_grid, brush_parse — public API only.
think First proof cut mirrored rung four (zero default-ground pixels) and panicked: the dark theme's ink IS Flax, the same color as the base sheet's paper, so counting Flax caught the ink glyphs — never zero. The two sheets swap paper/ink between the ground and ink roles.
think Reframed the proof honestly around roles, not a false zero — painted the same graph twice (light base, dark theme) and proved which color plays the majority ground vs the minority ink: under base, Flax is the ground and Bark only the ink; under the theme, Bark is the ground and Flax only the ink. The ground flipped wholesale.
obs Recolor proofs must respect color collisions — a paper↔ink swap means neither color reaches zero; the truthful measure is the ground/ink majority inversion, which is both airtight and honest.
obs Rung four themes the accent (head), rung six themes the ground (paper/ink); together they show both sides of the sheet are authorable from a plain .brush file, never Skate or Rye.
